e end  Kaplan recommends that investors if if  all the items the o UTS IDE don't come  out positive or at least there's no  negatives if there's any one of those  negative they pass on the deal in other  words they leave it outside other things  that biotech or venture capitalists will  look at in biotech  is what we call that an x-factor can  they put money into this company and in  five to seven years get ten times its  money back also things you want to look  at is the founders dilemma in other  words people who start these companies  be aware of what happens to their role  on the company as time goes on the 10 X  Factor all it really says is can we get  10 times our investment back in other  words for instance if we put in a  million dollars in a company this year  five years out can we get 10 million  dollars out of it in other words when  they sell the company when they do the  IPO with a percentage of stock that we  own you know can we get that much money  back now what why this is important is  that a lot of times investors don't talk  in terms of we want to insane things  like we want a 15% return on our  investment or we need a 15 percent IRR  in other words typically they'll say we  want a 5x or a 10x return well it's  important to note that that's what they  want and that's what they mean but also  it also points to if they can get a 10x  return in five years is that having to  invest any more money that's about a 60%  annual rate of return and for a lot of  people a lot of a lot of entrepreneurs  who haven't been dealing with venture  capitalists very much this is kind of  when they hear that that equalized  interest rate or internal rate of return  it tends to set them back a little bit  to think people need that kind of return  on their investment isn't that a little  high when you I can go to my bank and  get a loan for 5% or you know relatives  will will invest and only want like 10  or 15 percent annual rate of return but  there's a reason for that