arly and concise  but secondly isn't in the type of  industry is it in the same industry that  we invest in a lot of times one of the  quickest ways that investors will pass  and not invest in a company is they'll  simply say you know we're not in we're  not into the biotech space we're only in  software or or vice versa that'll be one  of the quickest ways they screen through  opportunities the second component of  the impacts which is tied to opportunity  is you know as the market large enough  to support substantial growth in other  words you know is this only a 50 million  dollar a year market or is it something  that could ultimately grow to a hundred  million or five hundred million or more  and is this a idea that might be able to  get you know five or ten percent market  share to create a sizable enough sales  base for the company and profits along  with it to support an investment in it  secondly you've got a look at within the  market growth opportunities not just the  size and how fast it's growing but also  can it support evaluation in other words  is this a profitable market that will be  able to if this company ever gets sold  or if it ever goes public I can support  a good stock price the pport is you know  does it generate positive present value  not just in financial terms but I unique  value proposition for customers  has it been accepted in the marketplace  or can you show that it's been accepted  not just through research but trial and  error and such will the customers buy  this again in biotech this might be a  little bit difficult because you have to  go through the through the preclinical  and so on phases the C portion of  impacts is is there any chance that  competition could come into this play  you know is there a intellectual  property that protects the technology or  are you far enough ahead of competition  that you can protect yourself and the  company won't be put out of existence  relatively quick is this a good time to  enter for the company why now why hasn't  this been done before is it too early  is it too late we have to answer those  questions and then finally associated  with the opportunity is the S or the  speed how quickly can this be done now  in general with non biotech companies  they may be looking at a time frame of a  year to three years can this be you know  can the technology be implemented that  quick or is it gonna be something that  takes too long for our investment  horizon now of course in drug discovery  biopharma and such you might be talking  a 10 or 15 year product development  cycle well can that be done on that  length of time but also can there be  alternative at least can you show  progress in the time horizon of the  investors now within the opportunity  impacts portion what capelin suggests is  that all of the Impa cts has to be  positive or at least no negatives if any  of those items are negative or don't  meet our criteria then we we say the  opportunity is not one we're gonna  pursue and we pass out and going down to  our next company the second area within  the outside approach outside impacts  approach is the uncertainty one of the  things that we have to identify is okay  how certain we are are we about the size  that the market will become or that  customers really will accept us and in  biotechnology that's really tough  because you know we have to try to look  out 10 and 15 years into the future to  see if people will really get at that  more importantly though it's not just  identify sort the uncertainties but are  there steps that we can take to help  manage it like can we align with certain  customers who will work with us in  developing the product are there types  of investors strategic investors who can  help offset some of the risk or provide  you know a certain type of of expertise  and you know how how understanding that  just what the uncertainties are but what  we can do to hopefully reduce the  uncertainties how is this gonna affect  the opportunity if for instance we find  out the market size is gonna be 50  million instead of 200 million do we  still have a chance to make enough  profit from an investment standpoint  so from uncertainty you go to the team I  think we people cover this a lot you  know you look at the things like the  previous experience of the team the  thing I really look for and I think a  lot of people a lot of investors do  within a management team of a new  biotech ventures  how passionate are they are they hungry  do they have you know have they put a  lot of time in this are they working  100% toward this venture  do they have some stock ownership or  have they invested themselves and to  realize that yeah we don't need to hire  everybody right away but what pieces  will need to be added and what type of  people will be needed to fill those  things and it's a company planning for  that so you've got opportunity  uncertainty the team the fourth and  fifth component is the strategy so are  we is the company have a strategy in  place that's is it aligned properly with  the opportunity the uncertainty of the  team and the exit does the team have the  experience to implement the strategy  from the investment standpoint you know  are they asking for enough or too little  money believe it or not it happens a lot  where new ventures don't ask for enough  money and it shows the lack of  experience of the founders of the  management team or if they don't ask it  they might be asking for too much and it  goes beyond what the investors can  handle believe it believe it or not also  that a lot of investors have a minimum  threshold especially venture capital  firms well they may say we have to put  in at least X amount of million into a  company to make it worthwhile otherwise  it's too small for us to manage in  addition to the investment side of it  you know what about the cash flow  forecasts do they have a for pro forma  cash flow forecast over three or five  years do they make sense you know if if  there's not going to be revenue right  away how are they going to manage their  expenses to control them you know to  make sure that they're spending enough  to push the product along on the 15 year  product lifecycle product development  cycle in biotech or or not gonna you  know that they're making  progress so all those things we looked  at in addition finally Kaplan recommends  that you look at the deal and the exit  strategy with the deal you're looking at  what type of financing is it is a  preferred stock is it a convertible loan  that can be changed from a debt you know  alone into into equity is a common stock  other incentives in place for the  management team and the investors so  everybody's kind of going along the same  wavelength is there government's  governance in place in other words you  know maybe there's a board seat that  should be added to this investment or or  what is that what is who is on the board  and how is the governance of the company  managed and finally what's in place to  manage the financial uncertainties and  especially in biotechnology where we can  talk incredible sums of money that need  to be invested are the investment are  they are they is the management team  asking for all the money up front  are they splitting it up into chunks or  tranches do they have the ability to  syndicate the investment so bring in  several investors not just one so all  those things have to be looked at it  from a deal standpoint not just the  percentage ownership at the valuation  and then finally the e is the exit you  know is this gonna be a company that can  do a public offering an IPO or is it  somebody who can be bought out you know  a merger and acquisition or is it gonna  be something it's just not big enough  that it'll ever want to exit or maybe  the entrepreneurs who start the company  don't want to go through a sale or an  IPO all those things have to be hashed  out and are part of an important part of  the deal so in the end  Kaplan recommends that investors if if  all the items the o UTS IDE don't come  out positive or at least there's no  negatives if there's any one of those  negative they pass on the deal in other  words they leave it outside other things  that biotech or venture capitalists will  look at in biotech  is what we call that an x-factor can  they put money into this company and in  five to seven years get ten times its  money back also things you want to look  at is the founders dilemma in other  words people who start these companies  be aware of what happens to their role  on the company as time goes on the 10 X  Factor all it really says is can we get  10 times our investment back in other  words for instance if we put in a  million dollars in a company this year  five years out can we get 10 million  dollars out of it in other words when  they sell the company when they do the  IPO with a percentage of stock that we  own you know can we get that much money  back now what why this is important is  that a lot of times investors don't talk  in terms of we want to insane things  like we want a 15% return on our  investment or we need a 15 percent IRR  in other words typically they'll say we  want a 5x or a 10x return well it's  important to note that that's what they  want and that's what they mean but also  it also points to if they can get a 10x  return in five years is that having to  invest any more money that's about a 60%  annual rate of return and for a lot of  people a lot of a lot of entrepreneurs  who haven't been dealing with venture  capitalists very much this is kind of  when they hear that that equalized  interest rate or internal rate of return  it tends to set them back a little bit  to think people need that kind of return  on their investment isn't that a little  high when you I can go to my bank and  get a loan for 5% or you know relatives  will will invest and only want like 10  or 15 percent annual rate of return but  there's a reason for that