 market  values or market potential in other  words they better be in industries that  might be able to create like a that the  overall market will be a billion dollars  in sales at some point in the future and  that this investment whoever owns that  will be able to get a good chunk of that  so all these things come into play  now a second thing that biotech  entrepreneur should really be concerned  with is something we call the founders  dilemma  the founders dilemma was a framework a  concept developed by a norm Wasserman  who was a professor at the Harvard  Business School now he's at at  university of southern california in his  research in the late 1990s early 2000s  he studied PI technology companies and  who eventually did IPOs initial public  offerings and wanted to find out what  happened to the management team as these  as these startups moved along and  eventually did the IPOs he analyzed the  few hundred different startups in the  u.s. he found one of the key things he  found and why he called it the founders  dilemma is that by the time ventures  were these ventures were 3 years old 50  percent of the founders of the companies  were no longer the CEOs in other words  investors had either fired him or they  had moved them into a less into a  position with less authority even in  year 4 only 40 percent were still in in  their corner office or 60 percent were  gone and then typically after the IPO  which happened after the fifth year only  25 percent of the founders remain so  ultimately Wasserman's found that four  out of five entrepreneurs typically are  forced out by investors from their CEO  post he also found as part of his  research and also this is correlated  with other researches and a couple other  journals is that usually entrepreneurs  even in high tech biotech companies  never make as much money or make just as  much money as the employees that work  for them so the misnomer that  entrepreneurs who start biotech  companies are going to be millionaires  and billionaires someday it's typically  not right Wasserman showed in other  research of 528 companies that over half  of the entrepreneurs made less  then at least one person who reported to  them and so things to conclude out of  this is that typically founders are  probably in biotech companies that  receive outside investment and I'm gonna  go down the path of an IPO or an  acquisition most founders are going to  be pushed out of their job secondly  they're not going to make a lot of money  additionally what wasserman tried to  identify what what the problem was he  said most entrepreneurs were too over  overconfident when they started their  company about what the process of  prospects were they were way too  confident and that they would be able to  stay CEO regardless of what happened and  be able to manage the business into the  long term now are any Cooper and other  researchers at you know University  correlated this this research by asking  3,000 entrepreneurs about what they  expected of their future prospects 81%  were confident of their own success and  that they would stay with their company  and over 60 almost 60% were were  confident of success in similar ventures  so in other words most entrepreneurs  especially in the biotech space tend to  be overconfident not realistic about  what their role will be in the long term  long term venture now what Wasserman  recommended and then what he part of  what he concluded from his research is  that investors and entrepreneurs must  realize that there is a control versus  wealth trade-off typically companies  that made that had higher value and also  the entrepreneurs were able to stick  around long enough the entrepreneurs had  to face the what they called the rich  versus king or queen trade-off in other  words did the entrepreneur just want to  be wealthy and want the company to be  wealthy or was control the major issue  did the entrepreneur want to stay you  know in control of the organization what  wasserman found out is that the  entrepreneurs who tended to be less  control oriented and more more more  concerned about creating wealth for  themselves and everybody associated with  the venture they tended to succeed in  the  venture tended to do well after the IPO  are better in the IPO he also found that  investors use a lot of mechanisms to  through control mechanisms to make sure  that they can maximize their wealth in  other words things like putting together  employment agreements for CEOs or being  on the board of directors and such and  then finally as part of the financing  agreements that's where this is  typically decided when doing investments  so that's kind of a brief overview of  you know just some things about  entrepreneurship a little bit of data on  biotechnology entrepreneurship and then  some research that's happened after the  fact what investors look for dilemmas  that are faced by founders in firms  including biotech firms and and and  other things associated with what  happens after a biotech startup moves  along the development path receives  money and and so on
 

